ice we had two  airings of the wisdom for life radio  show and we actually have the honor of  being the last people to do a live show  in studio at River West radio in part  because we showed up without knowing  that the station was officially closed  due to the Cova  pandemic we hadn't received the email so  it came in and we did our show I'll put  the links to those two shows below if  you want to listen to them and what else  we had our Milwaukee stoic fellowship  meeting we did it remotely using zoom  this time and I also did a workshop for  the stoah which is based in Toronto  Canada and I did a workshop specifically  on the virtue of fortitude and how it  helps us deal with failures and failings  so the video for that is available you  can see it below if you're interested in  that topic very important one that we  don't often talk about I think but but  plays a major role in any form of  intentional living so those were the  events I also did a couple podcast  interviews one with the stoic  psychologist and one with the practical  stoic links to those are also listed  below as well and I continued my work  with tutorials and philosophical  counseling and ethics consulting there's  actually a bit more demand for that  right now and you know my classes of  course all of you have heard I think  about the pivot to online so three of my  classes for this semester we're already  online now one of them is in rather  difficult circumstances because it's for  inmates in Wisconsin prisons it's part  of what's called the second-chance Pell  program which I might shoot a video  about that as well that would probably  be a good idea for me to do and  they're mostly in lockdown so so many of  my students can't communicate with me  their their modes of communication we're  already rather limited and we're just  trying to get them through the rest of  the semester and I give them a lot of  content that I load into their their  class Moodle shell so that they can have  a rich